1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,639
The test run finished. Did it pass? You looked at the screen, and you could tell.

2
00:00:05,189 --> 00:00:08,142
But your build server cannot look at a screen.

3
00:00:08,142 --> 00:00:11,031
It asks one question: what was the exit code?

4
00:00:11,031 --> 00:00:11,352
Zero,

5
00:00:11,352 --> 00:00:12,122
or not zero.

6
00:00:12,672 --> 00:00:15,900
And your manager does not want to scroll through a terminal.

7
00:00:15,900 --> 00:00:17,621
She wants a report she can open.

8
00:00:18,171 --> 00:00:21,308
Today you learn to run tests the way you need them,

9
00:00:21,308 --> 00:00:22,600
to read every result,

10
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:24,015
to trust the exit code,

11
00:00:24,015 --> 00:00:26,598
and to make a report that anyone can open.

12
00:00:28,148 --> 00:00:29,577
Here is what to expect.

13
00:00:30,127 --> 00:00:35,546
One: the ways to run tests. All of them, one file, one test, or only the ones you name.

14
00:00:36,096 --> 00:00:40,213
Two: how to read the output. The dots and letters, and the summary line.

15
00:00:40,763 --> 00:00:41,933
Three: exit codes.

16
00:00:41,933 --> 00:00:42,972
What zero means,

17
00:00:42,972 --> 00:00:43,947
what one means,

18
00:00:43,947 --> 00:00:46,481
and why every pipeline depends on them.

19
00:00:47,031 --> 00:00:52,705
Four: reports. A J unit file for machines, and an H T M L page for people.

20
00:00:53,255 --> 00:00:55,415
Then a live demo with pie test,

21
00:00:55,415 --> 00:00:59,456
and a real walkthrough of the H T M L report in a browser,

22
00:00:59,456 --> 00:01:00,361
part by part.

23
00:01:00,361 --> 00:01:02,173
And a lab with six checks.

24
00:01:04,223 --> 00:01:05,652
The ways to run tests.

25
00:01:06,202 --> 00:01:07,474
Just type pie test,

26
00:01:07,474 --> 00:01:10,019
and it finds every test in the folder.

27
00:01:10,019 --> 00:01:11,358
Give it a file name,

28
00:01:11,358 --> 00:01:12,831
and it runs that file.

29
00:01:12,831 --> 00:01:14,906
Add two colons and a test name,

30
00:01:14,906 --> 00:01:16,848
and it runs exactly one test.

31
00:01:17,398 --> 00:01:18,728
Dash K takes a word,

32
00:01:18,728 --> 00:01:21,588
and runs every test whose name contains it.

33
00:01:21,588 --> 00:01:23,052
Dash M takes a marker,

34
00:01:23,052 --> 00:01:25,846
like the ones you used in the last lesson.

35
00:01:26,396 --> 00:01:28,315
Dash X stops at the first failure.

36
00:01:28,315 --> 00:01:31,589
When you have a hundred tests and the first one is broken,

37
00:01:31,589 --> 00:01:33,734
there is no need to wait for the rest.

38
00:01:34,284 --> 00:01:36,979
Dash Q makes the output quiet and short.

39
00:01:36,979 --> 00:01:40,279
Dash V makes it verbose: one line for every test,

40
00:01:40,279 --> 00:01:41,559
with its full name.

41
00:01:42,109 --> 00:01:46,973
Choose the smallest run that answers your question. Fast feedback is the whole point.

42
00:01:48,523 --> 00:01:49,846
How to read the output.

43
00:01:50,396 --> 00:01:52,610
Every test shows one character.

44
00:01:52,610 --> 00:01:53,967
A dot means passed.

45
00:01:53,967 --> 00:01:55,753
A capital F means failed.

46
00:01:55,753 --> 00:01:57,467
A small s means skipped,

47
00:01:57,467 --> 00:01:58,252
on purpose.

48
00:01:58,252 --> 00:02:00,824
A small x means an expected failure.

49
00:02:00,824 --> 00:02:04,966
A capital E means an error: the test could not even start.

50
00:02:05,516 --> 00:02:08,410
After the characters comes the failure section.

51
00:02:08,410 --> 00:02:10,811
For each failure you see the test name,

52
00:02:10,811 --> 00:02:11,365
the code,

53
00:02:11,365 --> 00:02:13,028
and a line starting with E.

54
00:02:13,028 --> 00:02:15,737
That E line is the clue: what the code gave,

55
00:02:15,737 --> 00:02:17,399
and what the test expected.

56
00:02:17,949 --> 00:02:19,649
The last line is the summary.

57
00:02:19,649 --> 00:02:20,587
How many failed,

58
00:02:20,587 --> 00:02:21,525
how many passed,

59
00:02:21,525 --> 00:02:22,522
how many skipped,

60
00:02:22,522 --> 00:02:23,753
and how long it took.

61
00:02:23,753 --> 00:02:25,160
Look at that line first.

62
00:02:25,710 --> 00:02:31,427
Colors help. Red is a problem. Green is fine. Yellow is something you chose to set aside.

63
00:02:31,977 --> 00:02:33,499
A skip is not a failure,

64
00:02:33,499 --> 00:02:36,098
and an expected failure is not a failure.

65
00:02:36,098 --> 00:02:37,493
But neither is a pass.

66
00:02:37,493 --> 00:02:38,761
Keep an eye on them.

67
00:02:40,311 --> 00:02:43,170
Exit codes. The answer a machine understands.

68
00:02:43,720 --> 00:02:45,832
Every program ends with a number.

69
00:02:45,832 --> 00:02:47,048
Zero means success.

70
00:02:47,048 --> 00:02:49,672
Anything else means something went wrong.

71
00:02:49,672 --> 00:02:50,504
In the shell,

72
00:02:50,504 --> 00:02:55,752
the special variable dollar question mark holds the exit code of the last command.

73
00:02:56,302 --> 00:02:58,106
Pie test uses six codes.

74
00:02:58,106 --> 00:02:59,835
Zero: all tests passed.

75
00:02:59,835 --> 00:03:01,564
One: some tests failed.

76
00:03:01,564 --> 00:03:03,744
Two: the run was interrupted,

77
00:03:03,744 --> 00:03:05,774
for example with control C.

78
00:03:06,324 --> 00:03:07,935
Three: an internal error.

79
00:03:07,935 --> 00:03:10,191
Four: you used the command wrongly.

80
00:03:10,191 --> 00:03:12,318
Five: no tests were found at all.

81
00:03:12,868 --> 00:03:15,339
A pipeline does not read your output.

82
00:03:15,339 --> 00:03:16,942
It looks at this number.

83
00:03:16,942 --> 00:03:17,276
Zero,

84
00:03:17,276 --> 00:03:18,945
and the pipeline goes on.

85
00:03:18,945 --> 00:03:19,546
Not zero,

86
00:03:19,546 --> 00:03:20,414
and it stops,

87
00:03:20,414 --> 00:03:22,618
and nobody ships the broken code.

88
00:03:23,168 --> 00:03:25,593
That also means: never hide a failure.

89
00:03:25,593 --> 00:03:28,848
A run that prints failures but exits zero is a lie,

90
00:03:28,848 --> 00:03:30,954
and the pipeline will believe it.

91
00:03:32,504 --> 00:03:35,171
Reports. Three outputs, for three audiences.

92
00:03:35,721 --> 00:03:37,143
The terminal is for you,

93
00:03:37,143 --> 00:03:37,735
right now.

94
00:03:37,735 --> 00:03:38,447
It is quick,

95
00:03:38,447 --> 00:03:41,054
and it disappears when you close the window.

96
00:03:41,604 --> 00:03:44,100
A J unit X M L file is for machines.

97
00:03:44,100 --> 00:03:44,655
Jenkins,

98
00:03:44,655 --> 00:03:45,764
Git hub Actions,

99
00:03:45,764 --> 00:03:48,676
and almost every build tool understand it.

100
00:03:48,676 --> 00:03:52,143
They read it to show trends and to mark the build.

101
00:03:52,693 --> 00:03:55,002
An H T M L report is for people.

102
00:03:55,002 --> 00:03:55,652
One file,

103
00:03:55,652 --> 00:03:57,384
with colors and filters.

104
00:03:57,384 --> 00:03:59,550
You can attach it to a ticket,

105
00:03:59,550 --> 00:04:01,354
or e-mail it to the team.

106
00:04:01,904 --> 00:04:06,640
And the durations option lists the slowest tests, so you can see where the time goes.

107
00:04:07,190 --> 00:04:10,667
All of them come from the same run, with a few extra options.

108
00:04:12,217 --> 00:04:17,444
Go to the lab folder.

109
00:04:18,994 --> 00:04:20,892
Look around.

110
00:04:21,442 --> 00:04:24,878
A small warehouse project: the code in stock dot pie,

111
00:04:24,878 --> 00:04:26,045
a folder of tests,

112
00:04:26,045 --> 00:04:28,055
and an empty folder called out,

113
00:04:28,055 --> 00:04:29,741
where the reports will go.

114
00:04:30,291 --> 00:04:34,088
Run the tests.

115
00:04:34,638 --> 00:04:36,172
Look at the characters.

116
00:04:36,172 --> 00:04:36,572
A dot,

117
00:04:36,572 --> 00:04:38,639
then an s for the skipped test,

118
00:04:38,639 --> 00:04:40,973
then an x for the expected failure.

119
00:04:40,973 --> 00:04:41,840
Further down,

120
00:04:41,840 --> 00:04:42,173
an F.

121
00:04:42,173 --> 00:04:43,774
The summary: one failed,

122
00:04:43,774 --> 00:04:44,641
seven passed,

123
00:04:44,641 --> 00:04:45,441
one skipped,

124
00:04:45,441 --> 00:04:46,841
one expected failure.

125
00:04:47,391 --> 00:04:55,007
The exit code.

126
00:04:55,557 --> 00:04:55,807
One.

127
00:04:55,807 --> 00:04:57,368
At least one test failed.

128
00:04:57,368 --> 00:04:59,554
This is the number a pipeline sees.

129
00:04:59,554 --> 00:05:01,677
And it is saved in the out folder,

130
00:05:01,677 --> 00:05:02,426
for the lab.

131
00:05:02,976 --> 00:05:07,798
See the failure.

132
00:05:08,348 --> 00:05:08,907
The file,

133
00:05:08,907 --> 00:05:09,901
the line number,

134
00:05:09,901 --> 00:05:12,387
and the clue: assert three equals eight.

135
00:05:12,387 --> 00:05:13,629
The code gave three.

136
00:05:13,629 --> 00:05:14,748
We expected eight.

137
00:05:15,298 --> 00:05:19,628
Stop early.

138
00:05:20,178 --> 00:05:22,207
It stopped right after the failure,

139
00:05:22,207 --> 00:05:23,599
and says so: one failed,

140
00:05:23,599 --> 00:05:25,512
and only the tests before it ran.

141
00:05:26,062 --> 00:05:31,800
Run only some.

142
00:05:32,950 --> 00:05:33,597
Four tests,

143
00:05:33,597 --> 00:05:36,127
and only the ones with stock in their name.

144
00:05:36,127 --> 00:05:38,774
Verbose mode shows each name with its result.

145
00:05:39,324 --> 00:05:44,316
The slowest tests.

146
00:05:45,266 --> 00:05:47,505
The slowest test is the report test,

147
00:05:47,505 --> 00:05:48,997
four tenths of a second.

148
00:05:48,997 --> 00:05:51,112
Everything else is nearly instant.

149
00:05:51,662 --> 00:05:59,128
A J unit file.

150
00:05:59,678 --> 00:06:02,985
The screen looks the same. The difference is the file it wrote.

151
00:06:03,535 --> 00:06:07,012
Peek inside.

152
00:06:08,462 --> 00:06:13,134
Failures equals one. Any build tool can read this, count it, and mark the build.

153
00:06:13,684 --> 00:06:22,730
An H T M L report.

154
00:06:23,280 --> 00:06:28,784
Self contained means one single file, with its styles inside, so you can send it anywhere.

155
00:06:29,334 --> 00:06:31,062
Terminal output is for you,

156
00:06:31,062 --> 00:06:31,702
right now.

157
00:06:31,702 --> 00:06:34,326
A report is for everyone else: your team,

158
00:06:34,326 --> 00:06:35,158
your manager,

159
00:06:35,158 --> 00:06:36,246
the build server.

160
00:06:36,246 --> 00:06:38,550
So let us open the one we just made,

161
00:06:38,550 --> 00:06:39,382
in a browser.

162
00:06:39,932 --> 00:06:40,908
I open Chrome,

163
00:06:40,908 --> 00:06:42,999
and type the path to the file.

164
00:06:42,999 --> 00:06:43,347
File,

165
00:06:43,347 --> 00:06:43,766
colon,

166
00:06:43,766 --> 00:06:44,184
slash,

167
00:06:44,184 --> 00:06:44,602
slash,

168
00:06:44,602 --> 00:06:45,648
and the folder.

169
00:06:45,648 --> 00:06:49,830
A report like this is just a file on disk: no server needed.

170
00:06:50,380 --> 00:06:51,770
At the top: the title,

171
00:06:51,770 --> 00:06:54,801
and a line telling you when the report was made,

172
00:06:54,801 --> 00:06:55,937
and by which tool.

173
00:06:55,937 --> 00:06:57,011
Pie test H T M L,

174
00:06:57,011 --> 00:06:58,463
version four point two.

175
00:06:58,463 --> 00:06:58,842
Below,

176
00:06:58,842 --> 00:07:00,232
the Environment table.

177
00:07:00,232 --> 00:07:01,431
The Python version,

178
00:07:01,431 --> 00:07:02,252
the platform,

179
00:07:02,252 --> 00:07:03,326
and the packages,

180
00:07:03,326 --> 00:07:05,157
with the version of pie test.

181
00:07:05,157 --> 00:07:08,441
When a test fails on one machine but not on another,

182
00:07:08,441 --> 00:07:10,967
this table is where you start comparing.

183
00:07:11,517 --> 00:07:11,852
Next,

184
00:07:11,852 --> 00:07:12,656
the Summary.

185
00:07:12,656 --> 00:07:15,469
The first line says how long the run took.

186
00:07:15,469 --> 00:07:17,211
Then a row of check boxes,

187
00:07:17,211 --> 00:07:19,623
one for each kind of result: failed,

188
00:07:19,623 --> 00:07:20,092
passed,

189
00:07:20,092 --> 00:07:20,627
skipped,

190
00:07:20,627 --> 00:07:21,833
expected failures,

191
00:07:21,833 --> 00:07:23,039
unexpected passes,

192
00:07:23,039 --> 00:07:23,508
errors,

193
00:07:23,508 --> 00:07:24,245
and reruns.

194
00:07:24,245 --> 00:07:26,991
The number in front of each is its count.

195
00:07:26,991 --> 00:07:28,733
Red means look here first.

196
00:07:29,283 --> 00:07:29,693
Below,

197
00:07:29,693 --> 00:07:32,221
the table has one row for every test.

198
00:07:32,221 --> 00:07:32,836
Failures,

199
00:07:32,836 --> 00:07:36,798
expected failures and skips open their details by default,

200
00:07:36,798 --> 00:07:39,872
because those are the rows that need a human.

201
00:07:39,872 --> 00:07:41,717
The two links on the right,

202
00:07:41,717 --> 00:07:44,313
show all details and hide all details,

203
00:07:44,313 --> 00:07:46,499
open or close every row at once.

204
00:07:47,049 --> 00:07:47,960
Four columns.

205
00:07:47,960 --> 00:07:48,450
Result.

206
00:07:48,450 --> 00:07:49,921
The name of the test.

207
00:07:49,921 --> 00:07:50,832
Its duration.

208
00:07:50,832 --> 00:07:51,532
And links,

209
00:07:51,532 --> 00:07:55,385
which stay empty unless you attach logs or screenshots.

210
00:07:55,385 --> 00:07:57,417
The first row is our failure.

211
00:07:59,667 --> 00:08:01,845
The grey box holds the detail: E,

212
00:08:01,845 --> 00:08:03,561
assert three equals eight.

213
00:08:03,561 --> 00:08:06,002
The same clue we saw in the terminal.

214
00:08:06,002 --> 00:08:07,322
The code gave three,

215
00:08:07,322 --> 00:08:09,170
and the test expected eight.

216
00:08:09,170 --> 00:08:10,556
For a long traceback,

217
00:08:10,556 --> 00:08:11,678
you click expand,

218
00:08:11,678 --> 00:08:13,328
and the whole text opens.

219
00:08:13,328 --> 00:08:14,318
I click expand,

220
00:08:14,318 --> 00:08:15,902
and then collapse again.

221
00:08:25,652 --> 00:08:26,888
Below it: X failed,

222
00:08:26,888 --> 00:08:28,190
an expected failure,

223
00:08:28,190 --> 00:08:28,841
in orange.

224
00:08:28,841 --> 00:08:31,249
We marked that test as known to fail,

225
00:08:31,249 --> 00:08:33,136
so it does not break the run.

226
00:08:33,136 --> 00:08:35,804
Then skipped: a test we chose not to run,

227
00:08:35,804 --> 00:08:39,774
and the reason is stored with it: gift wrap is not built yet.

228
00:08:40,324 --> 00:08:42,361
The check boxes filter the list.

229
00:08:42,361 --> 00:08:43,507
I un-tick skipped,

230
00:08:43,507 --> 00:08:44,907
and those rows vanish.

231
00:08:44,907 --> 00:08:45,798
Tick it again,

232
00:08:45,798 --> 00:08:46,816
and they return.

233
00:08:46,816 --> 00:08:49,044
In a suite with thousands of tests,

234
00:08:49,044 --> 00:08:51,844
this is how you find the problem in seconds.

235
00:08:52,394 --> 00:08:54,502
Now I click the duration header,

236
00:08:54,502 --> 00:08:56,346
and the table sorts by time.

237
00:08:56,346 --> 00:08:58,716
The green rows are the passed tests.

238
00:08:58,716 --> 00:09:04,182
One more click puts the slowest first: the four hundred millisecond test is on top.

239
00:09:04,182 --> 00:09:06,817
The same answer as the durations option,

240
00:09:06,817 --> 00:09:07,541
but visual.

241
00:09:08,091 --> 00:09:09,281
That is the report.

242
00:09:09,281 --> 00:09:09,845
One file,

243
00:09:09,845 --> 00:09:12,226
and anyone can open it with a browser.

244
00:09:12,226 --> 00:09:14,106
You can attach it to a ticket,

245
00:09:14,106 --> 00:09:15,735
or keep it with the build.

246
00:09:15,735 --> 00:09:17,051
Back to the terminal.

247
00:09:32,501 --> 00:09:38,709
Find the bug.

248
00:09:39,259 --> 00:09:41,803
The restock function replaces the number,

249
00:09:41,803 --> 00:09:43,292
instead of adding to it.

250
00:09:43,292 --> 00:09:44,161
Five in stock,

251
00:09:44,161 --> 00:09:45,277
three more arrive,

252
00:09:45,277 --> 00:09:46,767
and the code says three,

253
00:09:46,767 --> 00:09:47,387
not eight.

254
00:09:47,937 --> 00:09:53,612
Fix it.

255
00:09:54,862 --> 00:09:57,102
Now restock adds to what is already there.

256
00:09:57,652 --> 00:10:00,873
Run again.

257
00:10:01,423 --> 00:10:05,562
All passed. The skipped and expected failure are still there, as planned.

258
00:10:06,112 --> 00:10:10,741
Exit code.

259
00:10:11,291 --> 00:10:13,318
Zero. The pipeline would go on.

260
00:10:13,868 --> 00:10:16,236
Run the judge.

261
00:10:16,786 --> 00:10:21,820
It checks every file in the out folder, and runs the tests itself. Six out of six.

262
00:10:22,370 --> 00:10:24,333
Clean up.

263
00:10:24,883 --> 00:10:26,739
Removed. Nothing left behind.

264
00:10:28,289 --> 00:10:32,449
Your lab task: run the tests, and save the evidence. Six checks.

265
00:10:32,999 --> 00:10:38,802
One: a J unit file of the failing run. Two: a self contained H T M L report.

266
00:10:39,352 --> 00:10:42,000
Three: the exit code of the failing run,

267
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:43,060
saved to a file.

268
00:10:43,060 --> 00:10:44,649
Four: the slowest tests,

269
00:10:44,649 --> 00:10:46,370
with the durations option.

270
00:10:46,920 --> 00:10:49,018
Five: run only the stock tests,

271
00:10:49,018 --> 00:10:49,694
verbosely,

272
00:10:49,694 --> 00:10:51,048
and save the output.

273
00:10:51,048 --> 00:10:53,010
Six: fix the bug in the code,

274
00:10:53,010 --> 00:10:54,160
not in the tests,

275
00:10:54,160 --> 00:10:55,987
and save the new exit code.

276
00:10:56,537 --> 00:10:58,862
Then lab check, until six out of six.

277
00:11:00,912 --> 00:11:03,707
Where to find the lab. Pause here, and write it down.

278
00:11:04,257 --> 00:11:05,721
One: the repository,

